problem:
The area of the shaded region is 108 square centimeters. All angles are right angles and all measurements are given in centimeters. What is the perimeter of the non-shaded region?

[asy]size(101);
filldraw(((0,0)--(0,6)--(8,6)--(8,-4)--(4,-4)--(4,0)--cycle^^(2,2)--(2,4)--(6,4)--(6,2)--cycle),gray(.6)+fillrule(1),linewidth(1));
label("$3 cm$",(5,-3),fontsize(10pt));
label("$5 cm$",(6.5,-4.7),fontsize(10pt));
label("$3 cm$",(2.5,3),fontsize(10pt));
label("$8 cm$",(4,6.7),fontsize(10pt));
label("$8 cm$",(9,1),fontsize(10pt));[/asy]

Isosceles triangle $DEF$ has been creased and folded so that vertex $D$ now rests at $D'$ on $\overline{EF}$ as shown.  If $ED' = 3$ and $D'F = 1,$ then find the length of crease $\overline{RS}.$

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair D, Dp, E, F, R, S;

D = 2*dir(30);
E = (0,0);
F = (4,0);
Dp = (3,0);
R = 7/4*dir(30);
S = F + 3/2*dir(150);

draw(E--F--S--R--cycle);
draw(R--Dp--S);
draw(R--D--S,dashed);

label("$D$", D, N);
label("$D'$", Dp, S);
label("$E$", E, SW);
label("$F$", F, SE);
label("$R$", R, NW);
label("$S$", S, NE);
[/asy]

Regular hexagon $ABCDEFG$ is inscribed in a circle with center $O$. A circle with center $A$ has arc $BF$. A circle with center $C$ has arc $BD$. If $AC = 6$ cm, what is the total number of square centimeters in the combined area of regions II and IV, rounded to the nearest integer?

[asy]
path a=(0,0)..(4,6.928)..(8,0);
path b=(0,0)..(8,0)..(4,-6.928);
draw(a);
draw(b);
draw((-6,0)--(6,0)--(3,5.2)--(-3,5.2)--(-6,0));
draw((-3,-5.2)--(3,-5.2)--(6,0));
label("$A$",(-6,0),W);
label("$B$",(-3,5.2),NW);
label("$C$",(3,5.2),NE);
label("$D$",(6,0),E);
label("$E$",(3,-5.2),SE);
label("$F$",(-3,-5.2),SW);
label("$G$",(-6,0),W);
label("$O$",(0,0),SE);
label("I",(-4.5,1.7),S);
label("II",(-0.7,2.3),S);
label("III",(1.5,1.8),N);
label("IV",(-1,-3.1),N);
label("V",(-4,-2.5),N);
[/asy]

In the diagram, quadrilateral $ABCD$ is a rectangle with side lengths 4 and 7. Point $E$ is the midpoint of diagonal $\overline{AC}$. Three semicircles are drawn with diameters $\overline{AB}$, $\overline{CD}$, and $\overline{EF}$, where $F$ is a point on $\overline{ED}$ such that arc $AEBF$ is also a semicircle. If the area of the shaded region is $20\pi$, what is the length of $\overline{FD}$?

[asy]
size(8cm);
fill(arc((4,0),2,0,180)--cycle,gray(0.7));
fill(arc((4,7),2,0,180)--cycle,gray(0.7));
fill(arc((0,3.5),3.5,0,180)--cycle,gray(0.7));
filldraw((0,0)--(4,0)--(4,7)--(0,7)--cycle,white);
draw(arc((4,0),2,0,180));
draw(arc((4,7),2,0,180));
draw(arc((0,3.5),3.5,0,180));
draw((0,0)--(4,0)--(4,7)--(0,7)--cycle);
dot((0,0));
dot((4,0));
dot((4,7));
dot((0,7));
dot((0,3.5));
dot((4,3.5));
dot((2,1.75));
label("$A$",(0,0),SW);
label("$B$",(4,0),SE);
label("$C$",(4,7),NE);
label("$D$",(0,7),NW);
label("$E$",(0,3.5),W);
label("$F$",(4,3.5),E);
[/asy]

Consider the graph of a function $y = h(x)$:

[asy]
import graph; size(8cm); real lsf=0.5; pen dps=linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10); defaultpen(dps); pen ds=black; real xmin=-3.25,xmax=5.25,ymin=-3.25,ymax=4.25;

pen cqcqcq=rgb(0.75,0.75,0.75);

/*grid*/ pen gs=linewidth(0.7)+cqcqcq+linetype("2 2"); real gx=1,gy=1;
for(real i=ceil(xmin/gx)*gx;i<=floor(xmax/gx)*gx;i+=gx) draw((i,ymin)--(i,ymax),gs); for(real i=ceil(ymin/gy)*gy;i<=floor(ymax/gy)*gy;i+=gy) draw((xmin,i)--(xmax,i),gs);

Label laxis; laxis.p=fontsize(10);

xaxis("",xmin,xmax,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true); yaxis("",ymin,ymax,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true);
real f1(real x){return (x+2)/(x+1);}
draw(graph(f1,-3.25,2.7),linewidth(1));
draw(graph(f1,3.2,5.25),linewidth(1));
clip((xmin,ymin)--(xmin,ymax)--(xmax,ymax)--(xmax,ymin)--cycle);
label("$y=h(x)$",(5.5,0.6),E);
[/asy]

Suppose we define another function by $k(x) = h(x+b)$. Based on the graph above, for what value of $b$ is it true that $k(x)$ is identical to its inverse, $k^{-1}(x)$?

Determine the value of x such that the expression
\frac{x^2+4}{x^3-3x^2+2x}
is not defined.

solution_start:
The only way to get to point $B$ is to go right five times and up four times. Therefore, there are $\binom{9}{4} = 9!/(5! 4!) = (9 \times 8 \times 7 \times 6)/(4 \times 3 \times 2) = 126$ total ways of choosing when to go up and when to go right.
[asy]
size(4cm,4cm);
int w=6;
int h=5;
int i;
pen p=fontsize(9);
for (i=0; i<h; ++i)
{
draw((0,i) -- (w-1,i));
}
for (i=0; i<w; ++i)
{
draw((i, 0)--(i,h-1));
}
label("$A$", (0,0), SW, p);
label("$B$", (w-1,h-1), NE, p);
[/asy]
The answer is $\boxed{126}

solution_end:
e only way to get to point $B$ is to go right five times and up four times. Therefore, there are $\binom{9}{4} = 9!/(5! 4!) = (9 \times 8 \times 7 \times 6)/(4 \times 3 \times 2) = 126$ total ways of choosing when to go up and when to go right.
[asy]
size(4cm,4cm);
int w=6;
int h=5;
int i;
pen p=fontsize(9);
for (i=0; i<h; ++i)
{
draw((0,i) -- (w-1,i));
}
for (i=0; i<w; ++i)
{
draw((i, 0)--(i,h-1));
}
label("$A$", (0,0), SW, p);
label("$B$", (w-1,h-1), NE, p);
[/asy]
The answer is $\boxed{126}.$

problem:
Two right triangles, $ABC$ and $ACD$, are joined as shown. Squares are drawn on four of the sides. The areas of three of the squares are 9, 16 and 36 square units. What is the number of square units in the area of the fourth square?

Note that the diagram is not drawn to scale.

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7));
draw((0,0)--(12,0)--(12,12)--(0,12)--cycle);
draw((2,12)--(2,14)--(0,14));
draw((0,12)--(0,17)--(12,12));
draw((0,17)--(5.8,31)--(12,12));
draw((5.8,31)--(24.8,37.2)--(31,18.2)--(12,12));
draw((0,17)--(-14,22.8)--(-8.2,36.8)--(5.8,31));
draw((0,12)--(-5,12)--(-5,17)--(0,17));
draw((1.2,16.5)--(1.7,17.7)--(0.5,18.2));
label("$A$",(12,12),SE);
label("$B$",(0,12),SW);
label("$C$",(0,17),NNW);
label("$D$",(5.8,31),N);
label("16",((0,17)+(-14,22.8)+(-8.2,36.8)+(5.8,31))/4);
label("9",(-5/2,6+17/2));
label("36",(6,6));
[/asy]

The sides of triangle $PQR$ are tangent to a circle with center $C$ as shown. Given that $\angle PQR = 63^\circ$ and $\angle QPR = 59^\circ$, find $\angle QRC$, in degrees.

[asy]

unitsize(1.0 cm);

pair Q, P, R, C;

Q = (2.43,3.46);

P = (0,0);

R = (4.43,0);

C = incenter(Q,P,R);

draw(Q--P--R--cycle);

draw(incircle(Q,P,R));

draw(R--C);

label("$Q$", Q, N);

label("$P$", P, SW);

label("$R$", R, SE);

label("$C$", C, N);

[/asy]

A 4-inch by 6-inch picture is enlarged for framing  by tripling its dimensions.  A 2-inch-wide border  is then placed around each side of the enlarged  picture, as shown.  Thin metal framing is sold only  in increments of one foot.  What is the minimum  number of linear feet of framing that must be  purchased to go around the perimeter of the border?

[asy]

draw((0,0)--(14,0)--(14,20)--(0,20)--cycle,linewidth(2));

draw((4,4)--(10,4)--(10,16)--(4,16)--cycle);

label("border",(7,17),N);

label("picture",(7,8),N);

label("frame",(14,5),E);

draw((17.5,7.5)--(14.5,7.5),Arrow);
draw((10.5,7.5)--(13.5,7.5),Arrow);

[/asy]

The figure shows a square in the interior of a regular hexagon.  The square and regular hexagon share a common side. What is the degree measure of $\angle ABC$? [asy]
size(150);
pair A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H;
A=(0,.866);
B=(.5,1.732);
C=(1.5,1.732);
D=(2,.866);
E=(1.5,0);
F=(.5,0);
G=(.5,1);
H=(1.5,1);
draw(A--B);
draw(B--C);
draw(C--D);
draw(D--E);
draw(E--F);
draw(F--A);
draw(F--G);
draw(G--H);
draw(H--E);
draw(D--H);
label("A", C, N);
label("B", D, E);
label("C", H, N);
[/asy]

If two lines $l$ and $m$ have equations $y = -x + 6$, and $y = -4x + 6$, what is the probability that a point randomly selected in the 1st quadrant and below $l$ will fall between $l$ and $m$? Express your answer as a decimal to the nearest hundredth.

[asy]
import cse5; import olympiad;
size(150);
add(grid(8,8));
draw((0,0)--(8,0),linewidth(1.2));
draw((0,0)--(0,8),linewidth(1.2));
label("$x$",(8,0),E);
label("$y$",(0,8),N);
draw((6,0)--(0,6)--(3/2,0));
label("$l$",(6,0)--(0,6),NE);
label("$m$",(0,6)--(3/2,0),NE);
[/asy]

$ABCDEFGH$ shown below is a cube with volume 1.  Find the volume of pyramid $ABCH$.

[asy]
import three;
triple A,B,C,D,EE,F,G,H;
A = (0,0,0);
B = (1,0,0);
C = (1,1,0);
D= (0,1,0);
EE = (0,0,1);
F = B+EE;
G = C + EE;
H = D + EE;
draw(B--C--D);
draw(B--A--D,dashed);
draw(EE--F--G--H--EE);
draw(A--EE,dashed);
draw(B--F);
draw(C--G);
draw(D--H);
label("$A$",A,S);
label("$B$",B,W);
label("$C$",C,S);
label("$D$",D,E);
label("$E$",EE,N);
label("$F$",F,W);
label("$G$",G,SW);
label("$H$",H,E);
[/asy]
